---
// Hand-authored inline SVG: how the FSA∩CMA overlap shape is measured by the
// even-odd rule instead of by winding direction. Inline (not an <img>) so it
// inherits the Broadsheet tokens from global.css and needs no client JS.
//
// Unlike RegionGraphNYC/RegionGraphChicago, this diagram encodes an *algorithm*,
// not seed data — there is no TOML to transcribe from. The source of truth is
// polygonArea + nestingDepth in the Atlas repo's
// api/internal/etl/ca/geom.go:
//   depth := number of other contours containing this one
//   depth % 2 == 0  ->  area added        (filled)
//   depth % 2 == 1  ->  area subtracted   (hole)
// The shape is deliberately drawn as ONE <path> with fill-rule="evenodd", so
// the browser fills it by the same rule the ETL measures it by — the picture
// and the code agree by construction rather than by my redrawing them to match.
//
// The three outlines are cast as a real, recognisable case: an FSA on the
// Ontario side of the cross-province Ottawa-Gatineau CMA (the metro the post
// already introduces earlier), the Ottawa River as the water inside it, and a
// real island in that river (Victoria / Chaudière). This is a SCHEMATIC of that
// geometry, NOT a traced StatsCan boundary — see the caption. It is here only
// to make the even-odd rule concrete; the picture is still a stylised shape.
//
// Two things a future editor must NOT "fix":
//   - Land and river are drawn wound the SAME direction on purpose: that is the
//     bug the passage describes, and the reason winding can't be the signal.
//   - The Ontario/Quebec split is decided by PRUID (province code), NOT by this
//     area math. It is deliberately out of frame; do not add a province line.
//
// Styles live in global.css (.post-diagram, .dg-*) per the one-stylesheet rule.
// Marker and aria IDs are prefixed dg-area-* — three diagrams share this page.
---

<figure class="post-figure post-diagram">
  <svg
    viewBox="0 0 680 400"
    role="img"
    aria-labelledby="dg-area-title dg-area-desc"
    class="dg"
  >
    <title id="dg-area-title">Measuring an FSA's overlap near the Ottawa River by nesting depth</title>
    <desc id="dg-area-desc">
      On the left, a schematic overlap shape near the Ottawa River: an outer land
      boundary — an FSA on the Ontario side of the Ottawa-Gatineau metro — with
      the Ottawa River as a water body inside it and Victoria Island in the river.
      Arrows on the land and the river show both outlines are drawn in the same
      direction, so the direction an outline is drawn in cannot distinguish a
      hole from a boundary. On the right, the rule that does work: the land sits
      inside zero other outlines, an even number, so its area is added; the river
      sits inside one outline, an odd number, so its area is subtracted; the
      island sits inside two, an even number, so its area is added.
    </desc>

    <defs>
      <marker
        id="dg-area-arrow"
        viewBox="0 0 10 10"
        refX="9"
        refY="5"
        markerWidth="6"
        markerHeight="6"
        orient="auto"
      >
        <path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 z" class="dg-arrowhead" />
      </marker>
    </defs>

    {/* ---- the overlap shape: one path, three subpaths, even-odd fill ----
         Rendered by the browser exactly as polygonArea sums it: land filled,
         river left as an unfilled hole, island filled back in. */}
    <path
      class="dg-poly"
      fill-rule="evenodd"
      d="M 55 305 L 40 195 L 85 110 L 165 62 L 285 60 L 360 105 L 385 195 L 355 285 L 265 335 L 145 330 Z
         M 108 175 L 140 132 L 185 118 L 240 128 L 295 165 L 322 215 L 300 250 L 250 258 L 195 240 L 145 212 Z
         M 192 205 L 188 172 L 222 160 L 250 180 L 238 208 Z"
    />

    {/* ---- each outline stroked separately so it can be pointed at ---- */}
    <path
      class="dg-ring"
      d="M 55 305 L 40 195 L 85 110 L 165 62 L 285 60 L 360 105 L 385 195 L 355 285 L 265 335 L 145 330 Z"
    />
    <path
      class="dg-ring dg-ring--hole"
      d="M 108 175 L 140 132 L 185 118 L 240 128 L 295 165 L 322 215 L 300 250 L 250 258 L 195 240 L 145 212 Z"
    />
    <path
      class="dg-ring"
      d="M 192 205 L 188 172 L 222 160 L 250 180 L 238 208 Z"
    />

    {/* ---- winding arrows: land and river point the same way, which is the point ---- */}
    <g class="dg-wind" fill="none" marker-end="url(#dg-area-arrow)">
      <path d="M 197 61 L 250 60" />
      <path d="M 150 128 L 205 120" />
    </g>

    {/* ---- role labels, sitting on the region each names ---- */}
    <text x="328" y="135" class="dg-sub">land</text>
    <text x="222" y="230" class="dg-sub">Ottawa River</text>
    <text x="210" y="150" class="dg-sub">Victoria Island</text>

    <text x="212" y="372" class="dg-note">
      The land and the river are wound the same way — direction can't tell them apart
    </text>

    {/* ---- the rule that does work ---- */}
    <text x="396" y="86" class="dg-note dg-note--start">
      so instead, count what each one sits inside:
    </text>

    <g>
      <circle cx="404" cy="120" r="5" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="418" y="124" class="dg-row">land — inside 0 outlines</text>
      <text x="418" y="142" class="dg-call dg-call--start">even → added</text>
    </g>

    <g>
      <circle cx="404" cy="192" r="5" class="dg-node dg-node--anchor" />
      <text x="418" y="196" class="dg-row">Ottawa River — inside 1 (the land)</text>
      <text x="418" y="214" class="dg-call dg-call--start">odd → subtracted</text>
    </g>

    <g>
      <circle cx="404" cy="264" r="5" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="418" y="268" class="dg-row">Victoria Island — inside 2 (land + river)</text>
      <text x="418" y="286" class="dg-call dg-call--start">even → added</text>
    </g>
  </svg>

  <figcaption>
    The shape on the left is drawn as a single path under the even-odd rule, so
    the browser fills it the same way the pipeline measures it: the land is
    solid, the Ottawa River is the hole inside it, and Victoria Island fills
    back in. It's a schematic near the river, not a traced boundary —
    counting nesting rather than reading winding direction is what makes the
    answer independent of how the clipping library happened to draw each outline.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
